<section id="guides" style="margin-top:35px"><div class="eyebrow">TruckFixHub starter information</div><h2>Make the next conversation useful</h2><p class="small">Editorial guides, separate from member discussions. These help you organize a problem and compare experiences; they do not identify a failed part or prescribe a repair.</p>
<details class="guide" open><summary>No-start: “won’t crank” or “cranks but won’t fire”?</summary><p>Those descriptions lead to different conversations. Start by describing exactly what happens when you turn the key.</p><ol><li><strong>No crank:</strong> does nothing happen, is there a click, or does the starter turn slowly? Note whether dash lights change and whether the symptom is intermittent.</li><li><strong>Cranks without starting:</strong> does it crank at its usual speed? Record dash warnings, exact codes, and whether the problem began after fueling, filter service, or another repair.</li><li><strong>Starts and stalls:</strong> say how long it runs and whether it happens cold, hot, or every time.</li><li>Include year, model, engine, recent work, and any measurements already obtained by a qualified technician. Don't jump directly to buying a starter or fuel pump from a similar story.</li></ol><p><strong>A useful title:</strong> “2016 Cascadia / DD15 — single click, no crank when hot.”</p><button class="btn secondary guide-ask" data-category="Engine &amp; starting">Ask about a starting problem</button></details>
<details class="guide"><summary>Coolant disappearing: describe where it goes</summary><p>“Losing coolant” can mean a visible leak, coolant being expelled, or an unexplained falling level. Similar symptoms can have different causes.</p><ul><li>Record how much coolant is lost and over what mileage or time, using the manufacturer's specified checking conditions.</li><li>Note visible wet areas, residue, temperature warnings, whether loss occurs under load, and recent cooling-system work.</li><li>If a shop has tested it, include the actual findings—not just “they checked everything.” Note fluid contamination observed by the technician.</li><li>Do not open a hot pressurized cooling system. Follow the manufacturer's shutdown guidance for overheating or coolant warnings.</li></ul><p><strong>A useful title:</strong> “Series 60 12.7 — coolant loss under load, no visible leak; looking for similar experiences.”</p><button class="btn secondary guide-ask" data-category="Cooling system">Ask about coolant loss</button></details>
<details class="guide"><summary>Fault codes: capture the whole code, not just the number</summary><p>A code helps frame the investigation. It does not, by itself, prove which part needs replacing.</p><ul><li>Include the engine model and year, reporting module, complete SPN/FMI or manufacturer code, and whether it is active or stored.</li><li>Copy the warning wording exactly. Mention other codes present at the same time and the order in which symptoms appeared.</li><li>Describe engine behavior: reduced power, speed restriction, failed regeneration, or normal operation with a warning.</li><li>Share actual technician findings and recent service. Avoid recommending emissions removal or bypasses; this community focuses on diagnosis and compliant repairs.</li></ul><p>Cover account details and personal information before uploading screenshots.</p><button class="btn secondary guide-ask" data-category="DEF, DPF &amp; aftertreatment">Ask about a fault code</button></details>
<details class="guide"><summary>Electrical trouble: what fails together?</summary><ul><li>List the affected systems and the conditions: rain, vibration, hot engine, overnight parking, or use of an accessory.</li><li>Include battery age, recent wiring or charging-system work, and any actual test readings with their test conditions.</li><li>Describe whether the fault comes and goes, and what changes when it happens.</li><li>Use the wiring and test information for your exact vehicle configuration. Similar-looking connectors or matching wire colors do not establish compatibility.</li></ul><p>A useful discussion separates observations from guesses. Do not bypass protective devices or interlocks to follow a forum suggestion.</p><button class="btn secondary guide-ask" data-category="Electrical">Ask about electrical trouble</button></details>
<details class="guide"><summary>Air and brakes: share details without road-testing a hazard</summary><p>Brake and low-air concerns require qualified evaluation. Follow manufacturer instructions and applicable operating rules before moving the truck.</p><ul><li>Describe warnings, pressure indications already observed, when the concern appeared, and recent air or brake service.</li><li>Explain whether the complaint involves slow pressure buildup, pressure loss, a noise, or a brake behavior change.</li><li>Include the truck configuration and verified shop findings. A forum reply is not clearance to drive.</li></ul><button class="btn secondary guide-ask" data-category="Air &amp; brakes">Ask about an air-system concern</button></details>
<details class="guide"><summary>Find a part without ordering the wrong one</summary><ul><li>Start with clear photos of the part, its label, connectors, mounting points, and the surrounding assembly.</li><li>Copy the existing number exactly, including prefixes and suffixes. A casting number may identify a casting rather than an orderable service part.</li><li>Include truck, engine, or transmission details relevant to that component. Use the full VIN or serial number privately with your supplier when needed.</li><li>Ask the supplier to verify supersessions, fitment, and what is included in a kit. Similar appearance is not proof that a part fits.</li></ul><p>Keep customer information, addresses, invoices, and full identifying documents out of public posts.</p><button class="btn secondary guide-ask" data-category="Parts identification">Ask for help identifying a part</button></details>
<details class="guide"><summary>Finish the story: tell everyone what actually fixed it</summary><p>The most valuable reply is the one that closes the loop.</p><ol><li>Open your discussion and use the <strong>What fixed it?</strong> field to record the outcome.</li><li>Explain the verified cause, work performed, and how the outcome was checked. Mention how long the truck has operated since the repair.</li><li>If the repair did not solve it, say so. Do not mark an untested suggestion or planned repair as a fix.</li><li>Other visitors can compare that experience with their own test results. The “Reported fix” tag means the original poster supplied an outcome, not that TruckFixHub independently verified it.</li></ol></details>
</section>
<section id="rules" class="panel" style="margin-top:30px"><div class="eyebrow">Keep the garage useful</div><h2>Community rules &amp; repair information notice</h2><ul><li>Share firsthand experiences and distinguish observations, measurements, and suggestions.</li><li>No harassment, spam, fake repair stories, illegal modifications, or unsafe bypass instructions. Disclose business interests and sponsorships.</li><li>Protect personal and customer information. Only upload material you have permission to share.</li><li>TruckFixHub may moderate or remove content. Use the report button on a discussion or reply to flag concerns for the site owner.</li></ul><p>Content is general information and member opinion. Accuracy, completeness, compatibility, and repair outcomes are not guaranteed. A similar symptom may have a different cause. Verify advice against the manufacturer's information for the exact vehicle, and use a qualified technician for diagnosis and repairs, especially safety-critical systems.</p><p class="small">Member posts are attributed to their authors. Editorial starter guides are identified separately. This notice does not promise immunity from liability or replace professionally reviewed terms.</p><h3>Privacy</h3><p class="small">Questions, replies, and display names are public. Your email is used for account access and is not displayed in discussions. The forum uses Supabase to manage accounts and store content. Sign-in sessions are stored in your browser. Do not publish sensitive information. This page has no advertising trackers.</p></section>
